WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.350
MARTIN OMANDER: Hey, Grant.

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:04.410
I'm developing a Cloud
Function and I'm having issues

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.602
troubleshooting it.

00:00:06.602 --> 00:00:09.060
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Oh, have you
tried debugging your function

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:10.680
locally?

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.240
MARTIN OMANDER:
Oh no, I haven't.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.600
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Let's
pair program together.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.610
So Martin, what's the issue?

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:27.800
Don't you write
perfect code that works

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.005
the first time you run it?

00:00:30.005 --> 00:00:31.370
MARTIN OMANDER: I wish.

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:32.870
Let me give you some background.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:35.630
I'm building a Cloud Function
to share all the cute animal

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:37.040
photos you sent me.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.740
Testing the function is
getting fairly complicated.

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:44.090
And I keep getting an error,
trying to fix it, deploy,

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:46.130
wait a minute, test again.

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:49.250
It's taking a lot of time.

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:53.250
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Yeah, that does
sound time-consuming and risky,

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.770
especially if you're
testing in production.

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:59.390
So let's go and I'll
check out your code

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:02.180
and your function
locally on my machine.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:03.920
MARTIN OMANDER: Sounds great.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.800
GRANT TIMMERMAN: So here,
I've checked out your code.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.420
You're a pretty
good coder, but--

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:10.200
sorry.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:10.700
Focus.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.020
Focus, Grant.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:14.000
Let me go and show
you how you can go

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.700
and run your function locally.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:19.400
So in this case, we
got a Node function.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.140
To run our function
locally, I'll

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:25.070
first install the Google
Cloud Functions Framework

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:27.447
with npm i @google
cloud/functions framework.

00:01:27.447 --> 00:01:28.530
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:30.380
I'm a pretty good coder, huh?

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.450
All right.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:33.500
So this is Node.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.110
But what about languages
other than Node?

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:38.700
My coworker, for
example, uses Java.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.610
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Oh,
great question, Martin.

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:42.530
So this library-- the
Functions Framework--

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:46.070
is available in all
Cloud Function runtimes.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:51.020
So the process is pretty similar
between Python, Go, Java, Ruby,

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:52.850
PHP, and .NET.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:56.150
In fact, there's a link in the
description of this video down

00:01:56.150 --> 00:02:00.370
below with documentation
with detailed instructions

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:01.470
for those languages.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:05.210
So next, we can go and just
use the Functions Framework

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:07.430
for that language
as a library, so you

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:10.350
can run your function locally.

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:15.150
Let's add a start script so
we can simply run npm start.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:19.140
This will start our localhost
server serving our function.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.380
MARTIN OMANDER: Neat.

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:22.440
So we can just curl
this localhost URL

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:23.790
and test the function locally?

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:24.290
Hm.

00:02:27.924 --> 00:02:29.700
Mhm.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.440
Oh, it looks like we're
getting the same error.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.210
Darn.

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:35.830
GRANT TIMMERMAN:
No, Martin, so this

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:37.985
is the error we want to see.

00:02:37.985 --> 00:02:39.610
It's actually the
same error that we're

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:41.050
seeing in production.

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:45.130
But now we can go
and test locally.

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:46.970
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, I see.

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:47.470
All right.

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:50.500
So how do we debug this locally?

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.950
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Well, you can
go use console.log statements,

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:55.740
which I sometimes do.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.730
Don't tell my manager.

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:00.880
But this can be a little
bit cumbersome, especially

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.410
with a larger application.

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:06.185
So let's setup
the Node debugger.

00:03:06.185 --> 00:03:07.560
MARTIN OMANDER:
That sounds good.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.480
Are you saying that I can
use the normal Node debugger?

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:11.500
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Yes.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:14.500
So Node comes with a
debugger out of the box.

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.840
We can simply
attach this debugger

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:19.820
to our Node process
running our function.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:24.490
So in VS Code, let's
use the Attach command.

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:26.320
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, nice.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:27.230
OK.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:27.730
All right.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:31.175
So oh, I see the status bar
change from blue to orange.

00:03:31.175 --> 00:03:32.050
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Yes.

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:34.120
So that indicates that
we've successfully

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:35.800
attached our debugger.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:40.802
Now let's add a breakpoint in
the beginning of our function.

00:03:40.802 --> 00:03:44.390
[VOCALIZING]

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:45.620
MARTIN OMANDER: Good, good.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:46.440
All right.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.560
And now let's run our
function, see if we

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.385
can catch this pesky error.

00:03:50.385 --> 00:03:51.510
GRANT TIMMERMAN: All right.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:56.588
I'll go and curl
our localhost URL.

00:03:56.588 --> 00:03:57.380
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:00.980
I see the editor has paused
execution on the breakpoint?

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:02.030
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Yeah.

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:05.090
In fact, if we go and
hover over some variables,

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:08.410
we can see the current values.

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:11.090
So let's go.

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:13.130
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, I see.

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:14.510
Oh, look at that.

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:18.200
Oh, it looks like
I used req.params

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:21.260
where it should be req.query.

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:22.580
Would you fix that, Grant?

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:22.970
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Oh, yeah.

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:23.685
Sure.

00:04:23.685 --> 00:04:25.380
It's a silly typo?

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:26.255
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah.

00:04:26.255 --> 00:04:28.910
GRANT TIMMERMAN: And it
looks like there's a few more

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:29.820
errors here.

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:32.510
I'll edit them.

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:35.520
And I'm just done.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.845
So now that we've
edited our code,

00:04:37.845 --> 00:04:43.580
we need to restart the
function for it to take effect.

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:44.180
One second.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:46.520
Let me go and just
cancel the process,

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:51.300
restart it, and try again.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.750
And boom.

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:55.000
MARTIN OMANDER: This is great.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:58.390
But Grant, the function
accesses the Firestore Database

00:04:58.390 --> 00:04:59.710
in the cloud.

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:01.840
How come that worked
when you ran the function

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.190
on your local machine?

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:05.110
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Oh,
great question, Martin.

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:07.360
So the client library
used in the function

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:10.490
picks up the local
credentials from gcloud.

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:15.860
In fact, I ran a special command
to get these credentials.

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:19.210
These are called application
default credentials.

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:22.620
These default credentials are
the exact same credentials

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:24.750
that you see with
Cloud Functions that's

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:26.710
used in production.

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:28.920
So to get this,
I ran the command

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:35.360
gcloud auth application-default
login before I set this.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:40.590
So remember that Firestore isn't
running on our local machine.

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:45.180
It's the function is using the
client library and our auth

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:49.650
to call the Firestore
Database on our cloud project.

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:51.760
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, I see.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:53.700
So Grant, how about
we close the loop

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:56.080
and deploy the code to
Google Cloud Functions?

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:57.330
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Sounds great.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:03.720
Let me run the deploy script
here, gcloud functions deploy.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:06.110
MARTIN OMANDER: All right,
it looks like it deployed.

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:07.680
And now let's curl the function.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:08.700
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Great.

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:13.330
Curling the function
with our URL.

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:16.620
And it looks like it works.

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:19.540
It looks like we don't see the
error of an undefined variable

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:20.710
anymore.

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:26.340
I'll click the photo link and
see if it actually worked.

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:29.873
Aw, look at that
cute puppy, Martin.

00:06:29.873 --> 00:06:32.950
MARTIN OMANDER:
[LAUGHS] Yes, so furry.

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:34.785
I'm happy the function works.

00:06:34.785 --> 00:06:36.910
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Well, it
sounds like we definitely

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:38.380
deserve a coffee break.

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:40.720
MARTIN OMANDER: Yes,
we've earned it.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:41.680
Great.

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:43.340
Thank you, everyone,
for watching.

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:45.580
If you have any questions
for me or Grant,

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:47.750
please add them in
the comments below.

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:49.450
Also, please let
us know if you have

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:51.430
another serverless
topic that you'd like

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:53.450
to see us cover in a video.

00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:54.520
Bye for now.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:57.870
[MUSIC PLAYING]

